---
Root Cause

The error RPC response exceeds maximum data length is not related to data size. It occurs because the notebook sets fs.hdfs.impl to the remote HDFS client, which overrides the hdfs:// scheme globally. When Spark executors write data back to the Hopsworks Feature Store, internal operations that use the hdfs:// scheme are intercepted by the remote client instead of the native HopsFS client. The remote client attempts a Kerberos SASL handshake with the HopsFS namenode (which uses a different authentication mechanism), causing the connection failure.

  Fix

  Register the remote client under its own URI scheme (rahdfs://) so it does not interfere with HopsFS operations.

  Step 1 — Add the following to the Jupyter Server Spark Properties:

  spark.hadoop.fs.rahdfs.impl=io.hops.hdfs.remote.client.HdfsRemoteFileSystem
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.core-site.path=/srv/hops/artifacts/core-site.xml
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.hdfs-site.path=/srv/hops/artifacts/hdfs-site.xml
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.security.token.service.use_ip=false
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.keytab.path=/srv/hops/artifacts/<YOUR_KEYTAB>      <-- UPDATE_ME
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.keytab.principal=<USER>@<REALM>                    <-- UPDATE_ME
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.krb5.realm=<REALM>                                 <-- UPDATE_ME
  spark.hadoop.rahdfs.krb5.kdc=<KDC_HOST>:88                            <-- UPDATE_ME

  Step 2 — Update the notebook:

  Remove this cell — it is no longer needed:
  hc = spark.sparkContext._jsc.hadoopConfiguration()
  hc.set("fs.hdfs.impl", "io.hops.hdfs.remote.client.HdfsRemoteFileSystem")

  All configuration is now handled by the Jupyter server properties above.

  Replace hdfs:// with rahdfs:// in your read paths:
  # Before
  sig_tran_aggr_read_df = spark.read.parquet("hdfs://bdp-cdp-qa-01-ns/...")

  # After
  sig_tran_aggr_read_df = spark.read.parquet("rahdfs://bdp-cdp-qa-01-ns/...")

  Everything else in the notebook (column selections, filters, Feature Store write) remains unchanged.

